Absolutely. The simulator should be treated as a **first-class downstream-system simulator**, not merely an account CRUD application. It should model the account, earmark, reservation, acknowledgement, and settlement lifecycle closely enough that your ACH application can be functionally tested without connecting to the real fund-management system.

# ACH Pre-Fund and Settlement Simulator

## 1. Objective

Build an enterprise-quality **ACH Pre-Fund and Settlement Simulator** that acts as a configurable downstream financial/account system for functional, integration, regression, resilience, and performance testing of the ACH Pre-Fund Reservation and Settlement application.

The simulator must provide:

* Account management
* Account balance management
* Available/ledger/reserved balance tracking
* Pre-fund reservation/earmark processing
* Reservation response simulation
* Vendor response/acknowledgement simulation
* Settlement processing
* Earmark completion/release/consumption
* Account transaction ledger
* Audit history
* Configurable success/failure behavior
* Delayed and asynchronous responses
* Duplicate/out-of-order response simulation
* CSV/JSON test-data loading
* Bulk account balance operations
* Test scenario management
* Operational UI
* Search and filtering
* Reconciliation
* Reset/seed functionality
* Integration with the ACH application through a configurable messaging adapter
* In-memory mode for local development
* Kafka or enterprise messaging integration for integration testing

The simulator must behave like a realistic financial system and must not be implemented as simple CRUD around a balance field.

